From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: tunable LM constants + confidence-gated ordering +
 auto queue-depth scaling + self-detected degradation decay + SAGA defaults

Applies ON TOP of adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch (kernel/addons/adios/
adios.sh must run first).

CHANGES FROM THE PREVIOUS REVISION OF THIS PATCH:
- REMOVED lm_seed_read/lm_seed_write: discovered upstream ADIOS 3.2.0
  already has lat_model_read/lat_model_write doing the exact same thing
  via sideload_latency_model(). The removed sysfs files were pure
  duplication; use the native ones instead for model persistence
  scripts (see adios_model_persist.sh).

NEW IN THIS REVISION (4 architectural additions, each wired into real
dispatch/completion logic, not inert sysfs):

1. Confidence-gated predictive reordering (model_is_confident()).
   Predictive reordering of async requests (the pred_lat addition in
   add_to_dl_tree) now requires BOTH the existing manual switch
   (compliance_flags/FIXORDER) AND a new automatic per-optype check:
   the model's small_count (already tracked upstream) must reach
   lm_confidence_samples (default 64) before its prediction is trusted
   for ordering. Below that, behaves like FIXORDER automatically, per
   optype, independent of the global switch. Targets the cold-start
   regression documented in prior testing (fresh/reset model making
   bad predictions were still fully trusted before this).

2. Auto-scaled batch_limit by real queue depth (q->nr_requests).
   Default batch_limit[read]/[write] are now scaled from the upstream
   base values by q->nr_requests relative to a baseline of 32 (roughly
   what the upstream defaults 36/72 were tuned against), clamped to
   [0.5x, 4x]. Portable across UFS/NVMe/SATA -- no device-specific
   probing. Directly prevents the batch_limit=128-on-a-QD32-device
   misconfiguration hit empirically on UFS 2.2 earlier today; manual
   sysfs override after boot still works as before.

3. Self-detected degradation -> accelerated decay (force_shrink_model,
   used in adios_completed_request). NOT a literal thermal-framework
   hook -- that would need a device-specific thermal zone name that
   can't be verified without the real device's kernel tree, and a
   wrong guess would silently never fire. Instead: tracks consecutive
   completions where actual latency overran the model's own prediction
   by more than lm_overrun_ratio_pct (default 150%). After
   lm_overrun_trigger_count (default 20) consecutive overruns, forces
   an out-of-band shrink (same shift-based decay upstream already uses
   via lm_shrink_resist, just triggered by symptom instead of volume
   threshold). Reacts to ANY sustained real-world slowdown (thermal,
   cache exhaustion, etc.) without needing to know the cause.

4. Device-class heuristic adjustment of global_latency_window. Honest
   scope note: does NOT identify UFS generation (2.2/3.1/4.0) -- see
   commit body in adios.c for why. Uses the same q->nr_requests signal
   as #2: non-rotational queues with nr_requests>=128 get a smaller
   window (3/4x), <=16 get a larger one (3/2x), on top of the existing
   rotational/non-rotational default split.

Also carries forward from the previous revision (unchanged):
  lm_block_size_threshold, lm_outlier_percentile, lm_interval_threshold_ms
  (sysfs-tunable versions of what were hardcoded #defines)
  SAGA default tuning: global_latency_window=30ms, bq_refill_below_ratio=50,
  compliance_flags=FIXORDER, batch_limit read/write base=64/128 (now also
  subject to the #2 auto-scale on top)

CORRECTION TO PRIOR TUNING-TABLE DOCUMENTATION: lm_shrink_resist semantics
were mis-stated as "higher = more aggressive decay" in earlier notes to
the user. Rereading the actual shift logic: value -= value >> reduction,
so a HIGHER reduction shifts away a SMALLER fraction per shrink event --
higher lm_shrink_resist is LESS aggressive (more resistant to forgetting
history), not more. Flagging this now since it affects how anyone reads
that tunable going forward.

NOT submitted upstream. NOT compiled/tested against a real android14-6.1
tree -- verified only for brace/paren balance and that atomic_t usage
matches an already-included header (ad->state uses it upstream). All 4
additions are new, not-yet-benchmarked mechanisms; their default
constants (64 samples, 150%, 20 overruns, 3/4x-3/2x window shift) are
reasoned starting points, not validated numbers. Test before trusting,
same as every patch before this one in this thread.
